1
00:02:51,030 --> 00:02:51,410
Peter Drucker.

2
00:02:51,670 --> 00:02:54,000
Thank you very much, Bob Hawkins.

3
00:02:54,580 --> 00:03:02,540
Ladies and gentlemen, let me say again how very happy I am to be once more back home

4
00:03:02,540 --> 00:03:11,800
at GBA and, by the way, in an act of rash optimism, I promise that I'll be back next

5
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:12,220
year.

6
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:23,840
But let me say, above all, how delighted and honored I am to have Dr. Friedman join us today.

7
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:33,300
I have been an admirer of his for, I think, oh, make it 30 years since he really, more than anybody else,

8
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:41,780
got the International Monetary Fund off the ground

9
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:47,380
and operating as the senior department head there for many years.

10
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,760
And my admiration for him has been greatly increased

11
00:03:51,760 --> 00:03:56,560
by his having written, with due respect to all other economists,

12
00:03:56,560 --> 00:04:00,140
the only book on inflation that's worth reading

13
00:04:00,140 --> 00:04:02,060
and worth reading more every day.

14
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:06,020
and so I'm very grateful

15
00:04:06,020 --> 00:04:08,840
Irving said you agreed to join us here today

16
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:11,760
and because of Dr. Friedman's presence

17
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:15,140
on the panel I shall restrain myself

18
00:04:15,140 --> 00:04:17,780
from making any comments on

19
00:04:17,780 --> 00:04:20,760
international monetary and financial matters

20
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:24,660
which would be sheer folly

21
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:27,340
in his presence and I leave that to you

22
00:04:27,340 --> 00:04:31,280
and I will talk about other elements

23
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:35,720
that are not more within my

24
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:40,970
but less within yours. And let me say

25
00:04:40,970 --> 00:04:45,370
it is very clear that we are

26
00:04:45,370 --> 00:04:49,580
in the midst of structural changes in the world

27
00:04:49,580 --> 00:04:53,360
economy. In many ways the

28
00:04:53,360 --> 00:04:57,840
disappearance of anything that could be called a key currency

29
00:04:57,840 --> 00:05:01,720
which was

30
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:14,000
quite clear, and the rapid shift away from the traditional industries as the main carriers

31
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,560
of international trade and so on.

32
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:24,360
And I want to focus today really on three and only three of these changes, all of them

33
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:32,320
structural, all of them hard to quantify and therefore unlikely to appear yet in the

34
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:42,350
economic models Bob Hawkins talked about, and all of them kind of contrary not just

35
00:05:42,350 --> 00:05:50,310
to conventional wisdom, but above all to conventional rhetoric, conventional modes of political

36
00:05:50,310 --> 00:05:52,990
and managerial behavior.

37
00:05:52,990 --> 00:05:56,790
And the first is the demographic shifts.

38
00:05:56,790 --> 00:06:03,010
And those of you who attended earlier seminars here in the last few years, we have been discussing

39
00:06:03,010 --> 00:06:12,430
them again and again. And so I will only say that fundamentally, we must accept the fact

40
00:06:12,430 --> 00:06:19,230
that in the developed countries, including the developed countries of the Soviet bloc,

41
00:06:19,690 --> 00:06:29,390
we face long decades of very great shortages of young people available for traditional

42
00:06:29,390 --> 00:06:38,470
work at any price, as a result both of the baby bust of the sharp decline in birth rates

43
00:06:38,470 --> 00:06:42,210
and of the sharp shift in education.

44
00:06:43,430 --> 00:06:52,510
And if you add the two together, we would find that the next 10 or 12 years in this

45
00:06:52,510 --> 00:07:01,270
country have no more than one-third of the entrant people available for entrant jobs

46
00:07:01,270 --> 00:07:07,070
in traditional employment, but one-third of what we had the last 20 years.

47
00:07:07,070 --> 00:07:12,810
This is also, of course, in part the result of the fact that the participation of women

48
00:07:12,810 --> 00:07:21,190
in the labor force cannot increase much more, because under 50, the labor force participation

49
00:07:21,190 --> 00:07:28,430
of women is already equal to that of men in any developed economy except Japan.

50
00:07:28,430 --> 00:07:34,070
So that from now on, what you will see as increased labor force participation will be

51
00:07:34,070 --> 00:07:36,150
a statistical illusion.

52
00:07:36,150 --> 00:07:43,090
As the older ones die off, who never were in the labor force, it will look as if the

53
00:07:43,090 --> 00:07:47,590
total labor force participation rate went up, but it doesn't.

54
00:07:47,590 --> 00:07:59,330
those who are in the labor force it is at its peak or at a peak can you this is on it isn't on

55
00:07:59,330 --> 00:08:13,560
no no okay can you hear me now this is on i'm sorry this thanks very much for telling how is

56
00:08:13,560 --> 00:08:20,480
this now okay and will you please not suffer in silence you suffered 10 minutes in silence which

57
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:23,800
is, I think, forbidden after age six months.

58
00:08:24,580 --> 00:08:27,960
So will you please scream and say louder?

59
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:29,660
Thanks.

60
00:08:30,280 --> 00:08:37,540
And so in all developed countries, we must expect the sharp drop in the number of entrance

61
00:08:37,540 --> 00:08:42,720
people, whether they are young or older women, is the material available for traditional

62
00:08:42,720 --> 00:08:48,940
work, which will incidentally cause very great turbulence because it will be accompanied

63
00:08:48,940 --> 00:08:58,000
by small but highly visible areas of high redundancy of older people

64
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:04,060
in decaying industries, whether that's Youngstown or Nagoya or what have you.

65
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:09,820
And at the same time, in the developing world,

66
00:09:10,960 --> 00:09:15,460
the very large number of babies who did not die in the 50s and 60s

67
00:09:15,460 --> 00:09:25,000
are now reaching maturity and need work, so you will see increasingly that the pattern

68
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:30,450
of traditional manufacturing will change.

69
00:09:30,450 --> 00:09:37,570
That part of it that can be automated, truly automated, will be automated simply because

70
00:09:37,570 --> 00:09:42,210
for the first time the hardware for automation is available.

71
00:09:42,210 --> 00:09:49,070
We have been talking automation 25 years, but we haven't seen much of it because it could be done on paper.

72
00:09:49,070 --> 00:09:59,570
Now it can be done in process as the mini processor is being integrated with the tool, with the machine, with the instrument.

73
00:09:59,570 --> 00:10:22,070
And this, incidentally, is likely to be as important an industrial revolution as the coming of the fractional horsepower motor was about 100 years ago, which really created the modern plant, because it made it possible to have modern power on the machine,

74
00:10:22,070 --> 00:10:27,110
so that you did not have to build your plant next to a generating station

75
00:10:27,110 --> 00:10:30,370
or did not have to have a generating station to have a plant.

76
00:10:32,010 --> 00:10:34,690
As long as you transmitted power with a pulley,

77
00:10:34,690 --> 00:10:40,350
you had to be within 100 yards of the source of power, or 200 yards.

78
00:10:40,890 --> 00:10:43,910
And so you will see real automation.

79
00:10:45,090 --> 00:10:51,850
And by the way, in this respect, this country is way behind.

80
00:10:52,070 --> 00:10:57,070
compared to both the Germans and the Japanese, who are really pushing.

81
00:10:57,070 --> 00:11:06,070
And on the other hand, where you cannot automate, you will see that labour-intensive stages of production

82
00:11:06,070 --> 00:11:13,070
will be done increasingly where the labour is, and that's not in the developed countries.

83
00:11:13,070 --> 00:11:21,520
And you will see production sharing in which the design, the management, the marketing

84
00:11:21,520 --> 00:11:29,460
are done in and by the developed country, and the actual labor-intensive stages are

85
00:11:29,460 --> 00:11:33,500
being done in every other place, growing rapidly.

86
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:35,620
It is already growing very fast.

87
00:11:35,620 --> 00:11:43,600
and this violates very much the way governments and labor unions and politicians

88
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:49,260
but also bankers and businessmen look at the international economy.

89
00:11:49,260 --> 00:11:55,000
We all still see in our mind's eye Adam Smith's complimentary trade

90
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:59,700
of English bullens against Portuguese wine as the model

91
00:11:59,700 --> 00:12:04,760
and that even though it ceased to be the model about 150 years ago.

92
00:12:04,760 --> 00:12:27,510
And that will increasingly be the wrong model. The right model are the shoes you and I are wearing, where the height came from an American cow and was tanned in southern Brazil, and the uppers were usually, the uppers are now being done in Haiti by a Japanese trading company, just to make things easy.

93
00:12:27,510 --> 00:12:42,190
And the soles are done in the British Virgin Islands. And the shoes are then assembled in Puerto Rico for the American market and, let's say, in Barbados for the European and British markets.

94
00:12:42,190 --> 00:12:47,290
and market designed in New York or in Zurich,

95
00:12:48,470 --> 00:12:50,750
marketed in the developed countries,

96
00:12:50,850 --> 00:12:52,750
which is where people wear shoes,

97
00:12:54,490 --> 00:13:00,150
and labor content, except for the cattle raising,

98
00:13:00,830 --> 00:13:05,070
is 60%, 70% developing countries,

99
00:13:05,990 --> 00:13:08,270
where were those shoes made?

100
00:13:08,270 --> 00:13:10,710
If you had to put on a label, made in,

101
00:13:10,850 --> 00:13:12,830
what would you write on it?

102
00:13:12,830 --> 00:13:37,270
And that is the pattern increasingly, and we are not yet organized, and least organized for it are the large, old multinationals who basically still see a 19th century pattern in which you have national subsidiaries, each of which is a parent company in miniature.

103
00:13:37,270 --> 00:13:42,810
and thus the whole range and everything for a limited market

104
00:13:42,810 --> 00:13:47,070
where tomorrow you will see plant facilities

105
00:13:47,070 --> 00:13:52,130
that do one or two operations for the world market.

106
00:13:52,830 --> 00:13:54,230
And we are not organized.

107
00:13:54,230 --> 00:14:02,370
Let me say that this implies a shift in the center of gravity of international business

108
00:14:02,370 --> 00:14:04,930
from capital to marketing.

109
00:14:06,220 --> 00:14:10,600
Tomorrow's international will not be controlled by capital.

110
00:14:10,600 --> 00:14:17,520
And incidentally, those of you who still feel that the demand of the Mexicans or the Brazilians

111
00:14:17,520 --> 00:14:23,000
that you have foreign partners is so, and the imposition,

112
00:14:23,480 --> 00:14:28,000
will you please accept the fact that for reasons which only they can understand,

113
00:14:28,380 --> 00:14:31,540
these poor countries are willing to subsidize you,

114
00:14:31,540 --> 00:14:36,890
and that they insist that you accept being subsidized for them.

115
00:14:37,310 --> 00:14:43,550
Insist, however, that from the beginning you build something that has an export market.

116
00:14:43,670 --> 00:14:45,750
In the first place, it won't grow any other way.

117
00:14:46,190 --> 00:14:48,630
Secondly, you won't have control any other way.

118
00:14:48,630 --> 00:14:52,510
And third, you won't really make a contribution any other way.

119
00:14:53,110 --> 00:14:57,430
The people in the developing countries who now reach working age

120
00:14:57,430 --> 00:15:03,350
cannot possibly find jobs supplying their own domestic market.

121
00:15:03,350 --> 00:15:08,950
Even in Brazil, it isn't big enough and not capable of growing fast enough.

122
00:15:09,370 --> 00:15:13,530
The only market is where the customers are and the purchasing power,

123
00:15:13,950 --> 00:15:15,830
which is in the developed world.

124
00:15:16,210 --> 00:15:19,750
So that's the only way to make a contribution today

125
00:15:19,750 --> 00:15:22,330
to the developing market countries

126
00:15:22,330 --> 00:15:28,710
is by creating jobs for export in labor-intensive stages of production.

127
00:15:28,710 --> 00:15:34,830
And tomorrow's multinational, as a result, is likely to be a confederation

128
00:15:34,830 --> 00:15:40,950
that's held together by control of marketing,

129
00:15:41,390 --> 00:15:49,710
where today's multinational sees itself very much in the image of an army division.

130
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:54,580
I have suggested, if you want a metaphor for tomorrow's,

131
00:15:55,200 --> 00:16:02,300
it'll be the symphony orchestra the conductor does not play the oboe and the

132
00:16:02,300 --> 00:16:07,500
oboist does not do what the conductor does but the conductor keeps the score

133
00:16:07,500 --> 00:16:14,140
and the conductor decides whether we play Beethoven or Bartok and that is

134
00:16:14,140 --> 00:16:20,540
tomorrow's multinational but the oboist better play the oboe and not the

135
00:16:20,540 --> 00:16:25,260
conductor I have at least never met a conductor whom I would entrust an oboe

136
00:16:25,260 --> 00:16:34,620
and so that is tomorrow's multinational and the second thing to say is that you

137
00:16:34,620 --> 00:16:39,800
know all of us have been talking about the last ten years being years of

138
00:16:39,800 --> 00:16:48,300
stagnation but that is not true there have been years of stagnation in the

139
00:16:48,300 --> 00:16:53,620
developing developed countries and by the way very unevenly in this country we

140
00:16:53,620 --> 00:17:00,340
We had fairly substantial growth, basically, if I sound critical, I think we had growth

141
00:17:00,340 --> 00:17:03,200
where we shouldn't have had it.

142
00:17:03,200 --> 00:17:05,500
Ours was not the healthy development.

143
00:17:05,500 --> 00:17:10,300
We had growth in old industries, not the new ones, basically.

144
00:17:10,300 --> 00:17:14,300
But fairly substantial growth.

145
00:17:14,300 --> 00:17:23,120
When Japan and Germany was there, very successful exploitation of the world market had no better

146
00:17:23,120 --> 00:17:30,220
growth than we in some areas. But that is not the point. The point is that a whole group

147
00:17:30,220 --> 00:17:39,380
of countries in this very difficult period had extraordinary fast growth in the industries

148
00:17:39,380 --> 00:17:45,720
that are right for them. We have a whole new group in the world economy, whether you call

149
00:17:45,720 --> 00:17:52,820
them the rapidly industrializing countries or the almost developed ones, Brazil and Mexico

150
00:17:52,820 --> 00:17:55,000
and perhaps Colombia, close to it.

151
00:17:55,420 --> 00:18:02,040
And, of course, the non-communist Chinese communities of Taiwan, Hong Kong, and Singapore.

152
00:18:02,780 --> 00:18:09,990
By the way, I'm one of those who do not believe in the economic growth of Red China ahead of us.

153
00:18:09,990 --> 00:18:14,490
I think Red China faces nothing but serious trouble the next 20 years.

154
00:18:15,610 --> 00:18:16,970
But that's a footnote.

155
00:18:18,270 --> 00:18:25,270
And maybe the Mediterranean.

156
00:18:26,300 --> 00:18:32,620
These are almost developed countries, and my definition of an almost developed country

157
00:18:32,620 --> 00:18:37,760
is a country that can do any one thing well, but not many things.

158
00:18:39,340 --> 00:18:45,940
And these are the countries in which, in some of them, you have a combination in Brazil

159
00:18:45,940 --> 00:18:52,220
of very large labor surpluses with substantial managerial competence,

160
00:18:52,220 --> 00:18:56,520
And Brazil is alone a substantial domestic market.

161
00:18:57,240 --> 00:19:02,200
In others like Mexico, you have the same without a substantial domestic market.

162
00:19:02,760 --> 00:19:06,120
In the Chinese areas, you have no labor surplus.

163
00:19:06,280 --> 00:19:12,140
Basically, they will increasingly have to move from labor-intensive industries,

164
00:19:12,300 --> 00:19:16,720
even South Korea, into more knowledge-intensive ones.

165
00:19:16,720 --> 00:19:33,500
But they all have in common that they are now becoming factors on the world market, that they are moving from a stage in which their development was capital-paced to one where it's export-paced.

166
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:50,830
They are all now moving into an export-led stage of development, and so they are all going to become major exporters, though probably not of finished goods, but of stages of production in many cases.

167
00:19:51,610 --> 00:19:58,210
And at least the Brazilians, who in some ways are the most intelligent of the lot, see this quite clearly.

168
00:19:58,210 --> 00:20:05,710
This is a different pattern from the Japanese, which was not export-based largely,

169
00:20:05,710 --> 00:20:10,710
till Japan was fully developed, it was based on the building of a domestic market,

170
00:20:10,710 --> 00:20:16,710
and it was based on bringing in technology and making the whole product.

171
00:20:16,710 --> 00:20:20,710
Brazil does not behave that way.

172
00:20:20,710 --> 00:20:26,710
They bring in technology, yes, but primarily they bring in stages of production

173
00:20:26,710 --> 00:20:30,630
and they have been looking for export markets

174
00:20:30,630 --> 00:20:34,030
and I think this is in view of their demographics

175
00:20:34,030 --> 00:20:36,230
their right policy for them

176
00:20:36,230 --> 00:20:40,330
but it's creating quite new opportunities

177
00:20:40,330 --> 00:20:42,790
on the world market and quite new stresses

178
00:20:42,790 --> 00:20:47,630
and I think that the almost developing countries

179
00:20:47,630 --> 00:20:51,110
are the most interesting part of the world economy

180
00:20:51,110 --> 00:20:55,750
they have been showing tremendous growth potential

181
00:20:55,750 --> 00:21:06,030
at a very difficult time, because they were most severely hit by OPEC, and yet they have done.

182
00:21:06,790 --> 00:21:13,090
And the third thing which I shall talk about, and here I'm going out on a limb,

183
00:21:13,090 --> 00:21:26,310
which I promptly will then saw off behind me, is I think one can say with something like 75% probability

184
00:21:26,310 --> 00:21:36,120
that the terms of trade will not improve in favor of the raw material producing countries

185
00:21:36,120 --> 00:21:42,290
except for the major food producers, but will continue to deteriorate.

186
00:21:42,290 --> 00:21:49,220
You know, if you look as a non-economist, which I am, I'm a consumer of economics, not a producer.

187
00:21:49,800 --> 00:21:57,640
If you look at economics, one of the things that underlies the 20th century crisis of economics

188
00:21:57,640 --> 00:22:02,760
and some of you know that I was a member of the original Keynes Seminar,

189
00:22:02,940 --> 00:22:05,220
so I've been in crisis a long time,

190
00:22:08,240 --> 00:22:14,340
is the fact that the basic assumption the 19th century economists made axiomatically,

191
00:22:15,880 --> 00:22:24,340
that as you industrialize, you lose the capacity for effective, efficient, and competitive food production,

192
00:22:24,340 --> 00:22:29,680
which was a generalization of the British and Belgian models.

193
00:22:30,140 --> 00:22:38,060
that that was no longer true. That on the contrary, the more highly advanced industrially a country,

194
00:22:38,060 --> 00:22:45,900
the more productive its agriculture. Even the British one has recovered from the low point of

195
00:22:45,900 --> 00:22:53,420
late Victorian agriculture in terms both of total production and productivity, even though it was

196
00:22:53,420 --> 00:23:00,220
and for so long sacrificed to the political expediency and considerations of the empire.

197
00:23:01,660 --> 00:23:10,380
But it's no longer true. Precisely because agriculture has become both the most capital

198
00:23:10,380 --> 00:23:19,500
intensive and the most knowledge intensive of all our major economic sectors, it is most productive

199
00:23:19,500 --> 00:23:24,960
in highly developed countries such as the United States and Canada, which to a large

200
00:23:24,960 --> 00:23:29,000
extent underlies the Northwest tension.

201
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:34,860
And beginning with 1900 or so, interrupted by two world wars, the terms of trade have

202
00:23:34,860 --> 00:23:43,120
steadily drifted against countries that are primarily raw material producers.

203
00:23:43,120 --> 00:23:50,820
In fact, I think one can say that the only way for a country to have advantageous terms

204
00:23:50,820 --> 00:23:56,740
of trade is to have the knowledge base which only a modern industrial establishment can

205
00:23:56,740 --> 00:23:58,270
give you.

206
00:23:58,270 --> 00:24:06,810
This, I think, even goes for the OPEC countries, whose future I do not view with great optimism,

207
00:24:06,810 --> 00:24:13,110
not for any length of time, politically, socially, or economically.

208
00:24:13,110 --> 00:24:23,730
But except for situations where you can have a temporary monopoly, the rest of the pure

209
00:24:23,730 --> 00:24:30,810
raw materials, I think, are likely to have a continuing downward drift in their terms

210
00:24:30,810 --> 00:24:42,560
of trade, whether that's metals or minerals or forest products, for reasons which I do

211
00:24:42,560 --> 00:24:53,320
I do not quite understand, but I think that the interruptions of this trend are temporary.

212
00:24:53,320 --> 00:25:04,320
They are mostly wars or periods of high confusion, such as panic, and then you get this sudden

213
00:25:04,320 --> 00:25:11,320
inflationary boom, followed by collapse, such as we have seen in almost any major commodity

214
00:25:11,320 --> 00:25:16,140
commodities the last five years the small thing is to stay out of the boom

215
00:25:16,140 --> 00:25:22,900
then and I think that if you plot it on a secular basis you will see a

216
00:25:22,900 --> 00:25:31,040
continuing downward drift of the terms of trade largely because the marketing

217
00:25:31,040 --> 00:25:36,880
the purchasing power of the developed world is so great and this will be

218
00:25:36,880 --> 00:25:46,760
accelerated by automation. And so the last thing I would say is that I foresee a widening of the

219
00:25:46,760 --> 00:25:54,400
north-south gap rather than a narrowing, no matter what aid policies there are, what transfer

220
00:25:54,400 --> 00:26:02,020
policies there are, except for the almost developed countries as they develop their knowledge base

221
00:26:02,020 --> 00:26:11,300
and the ability to market not so much products as the results of their labor,

222
00:26:11,440 --> 00:26:16,260
their abundant labor stages of production in the developed countries

223
00:26:16,260 --> 00:26:19,760
and then build their own domestic markets.

224
00:26:20,180 --> 00:26:23,440
These, I think, are some of the things to say.

225
00:26:23,940 --> 00:26:27,720
Let me say to me the most important single factor

226
00:26:27,720 --> 00:26:31,740
from the point of view of the structure of the world economy

227
00:26:31,740 --> 00:26:35,340
is the diminishing role of capital,

228
00:26:35,980 --> 00:26:40,260
even though capital will be increasingly in short supply.

229
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:44,060
Capital formation today is totally inadequate,

230
00:26:44,620 --> 00:26:46,880
as it always is in an inflationary period.

231
00:26:47,400 --> 00:26:51,340
After all, inflation is the destruction of capital by government.

232
00:26:52,120 --> 00:26:57,120
And it's one of the few things government is good at.

233
00:26:57,120 --> 00:27:01,300
And very good at it.

234
00:27:01,300 --> 00:27:22,320
And so we have inadequate capital formation, aggravated, let me say, by the fact that totally unlike the Keynesian assumption, we have built into the modern economy an under-savings tendency based on our age structure,

235
00:27:22,320 --> 00:27:25,760
based on the fact that everybody in this room

236
00:27:25,760 --> 00:27:31,260
saves some 25% to 30% of his income

237
00:27:31,260 --> 00:27:35,300
and not a penny of it becomes capital formation.

238
00:27:36,240 --> 00:27:40,360
Social Security, 12% of your salary,

239
00:27:40,540 --> 00:27:43,320
up to 18,000 or 25,000 now,

240
00:27:43,740 --> 00:27:46,480
goes out immediately as consumption.

241
00:27:47,710 --> 00:27:50,870
And what you put into the pension plan of your company,

242
00:27:50,870 --> 00:28:04,900
About 70% of it already goes out, and then the rest of the saving of most of us is the mortgage on the house, and that the house is not capital.

243
00:28:05,660 --> 00:28:15,060
The house is durable consumer goods with a good resale value, but it is not the productive asset in the economy's sense.

244
00:28:15,060 --> 00:28:31,540
So here is something like 40% of the typical wage and salary recipient income in developed countries that he looks upon as savings, and therefore he does not see any need to save more.

245
00:28:31,540 --> 00:29:00,570
If you plot capital accumulation rates in relation to social security, then there is no great difference between Japan and this country. The high rate of capital formation in Japan is an illusion. The Japanese have no social security payment and very practically nothing per way of a pension plan and almost even less per way of a mortgage than Arnold Homo has.

246
00:29:00,570 --> 00:29:03,730
and if you adjust for that

247
00:29:03,730 --> 00:29:04,810
then the Japanese

248
00:29:04,810 --> 00:29:06,810
form more capital than we do

249
00:29:06,810 --> 00:29:07,890
but not much more

250
00:29:07,890 --> 00:29:09,410
nor do the Germans

251
00:29:09,410 --> 00:29:10,990
the Germans are the highest probably

252
00:29:10,990 --> 00:29:13,110
so basically

253
00:29:13,110 --> 00:29:14,690
we are trading

254
00:29:14,690 --> 00:29:17,800
what may have been

255
00:29:17,800 --> 00:29:19,440
genuine capital formation

256
00:29:19,440 --> 00:29:21,340
I've never been convinced

257
00:29:21,340 --> 00:29:23,360
that the economist really knows

258
00:29:23,360 --> 00:29:24,400
how to measure it

259
00:29:24,400 --> 00:29:26,860
against things that are clearly

260
00:29:26,860 --> 00:29:29,480
savings but not capital formation

261
00:29:29,480 --> 00:29:36,480
so there's a built-in tendency towards under saving in any moment
